WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.430
So here we have tin, but this is a different
sample of tin from

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.970
what people might be used to because most
people see tin in

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.940
foil, or they think it is tin foil that they
use to wrap up the

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.360
chicken, mostly that is aluminium foil.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.760
Tin has the symbol Sn because it comes from
the Latin word

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.270
stannum which means tin and in fact if you
go to Devon in

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.850
the southwest of England you will find that
some of the

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.789
towns there are called stannary towns because
that’s where

00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:36.719
they used to mine and trade in tin. Tin has
a very old role in

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:41.460
trade and in technology. Bronze which people
used in the old

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:48.329
days was an alloy of tin and copper which
gave better swords

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.539
than copper, so if you were fighting with
somebody who had

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:56.100
a copper sword and you had a bronze one, you
won, at least

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.959
if you were quick.

00:00:57.959 --> 00:01:00.389
Here we see a beautiful sample of tin metal
and you can see it

00:01:00.389 --> 00:01:04.949
is a really beautiful rod and it is packaged
in glass to stop

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:09.149
oxidation, but you can see a beautiful silvery
shiny metal.

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:14.690
Tin has also been used very widely for coating
the insides of

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:20.960
iron and steel containers for food, that’s
why they are called

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.520
tin even though they were made from iron but
they had a

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.229
thin coating of tin on the surface, and tin
is also used very

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:34.399
widely for solder, for putting together metal
components or

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:36.119
electronic components.

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:40.969
Lovely sample of tin, so we will pop that
back in and back

00:01:40.969 --> 00:01:41.409
into the drawer.

